{"level":30,"time":1778273160455,"pid":56878,"hostname":"Mac.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"13.md","index":2,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FcoD2msPjqh6Xp9LcQ6aqQ","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Transformer Pad Clearances and Location Requirements\n     2→\n     3→## Description\n     4→\n     5→This grouping covers spatial and location requirements for transformer pad installations, including horizontal clearances from buildings and site features, vertical clearances for equipment access and overhead obstructions, separation from underground utilities, property location restrictions and prohibited areas, and transformer pad sizing and protection standards.\n     6→\n     7→## Regulatory Overview\n     8→\n     9→Transformer pad clearances are governed by a multi-layered framework in the Utilities Criteria Manual (UCM) that addresses horizontal clearances from structures and site features, vertical clearances for crane access and overhead obstructions, separation from underground utilities, and property/easement location restrictions. The primary regulatory structure is UCM § 1.10.4 Table 1.10.4, which establishes clearance requirements based on equipment orientation, building materials, and spatial configuration.\n    10→\n    11→**Pad-Mounted Transformers:** Electrical transformers installed at ground level on concrete foundation pads (as distinguished from pole-mounted or vault transformers). They contain pad-locked access doors and are supplied in both single-phase and three-phase configurations. Specific pad dimensions are determined by Austin Energy Design on a project-specific basis, not standardized in code.\n    12→\n    13→**Three-Phase vs. Single-Phase Service:** Service type is determined by Austin Energy Design based on customer electrical load requirements and infrastructure availability, not from civil site plans alone. Three-phase service is furnished only where the customer's