{"level":30,"time":1778273662689,"pid":56878,"hostname":"Mac.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0141xUbDH4kFTHTUazUEduhw","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Consolidated Site Plan Application\n     2→\n     3→Completeness check submittal for a consolidated site plan permit application in the City of Austin.\n     4→\n     5→## Sections\n     6→\n     7→1. **Project Information** (pp. 1-2)\n     8→   Details the project type, name, street address, proposed development description, and legal deed references.\n     9→   File: `01-project-information.md`\n    10→2. **Contacts Information** (pp. 2-3)\n    11→   Provides contact details for the applicant, owner, engineer, and other professionals involved in the project.\n    12→   File: `02-contacts-information.md`\n    13→3. **Property Attributes** (pp. 3-4)\n    14→   Outlines jurisdictional boundaries, housing program participation, watershed details, and street/neighborhood plans for the site.\n    15→   File: `03-property-attributes.md`\n    16→4. **Application Assessment and Site Area** (p. 5)\n    17→   Contains traffic impact analysis requirements, site area calculations, existing/proposed zoning use tables, and impervious cover percentages.\n    18→   File: `04-application-assessment-and-site-area.md`\n    19→5. **Land Use, Waivers, and Arborist Review** (p. 6)\n    20→   Details compatibility standards, requested waivers or variances, and tree counts within and adjacent to the limits of construction.\n    21→   File: `05-land-use-waivers-and-arborist-review.md`\n    22→6. **Authorizations and Acknowledgments** (pp. 7-8)\n    23→   Contains applicant signatures for submittal verification, inspection authorization, and general acknowledgments regarding property restrictions.\n    24→   File: `06-authorizations-and-acknowledgments.md`\n    25→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"90431bd8-bd79-4eb9-bd46-3969546723e7","uuid":"05c96f79-2d69-4878-92d7-08918fa644e3","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/supplementary-docs/consolidated-site-plan-application/overview.md","content":"# Consolidated Site Plan Application\n\nCompleteness check submittal for a consolidated site plan permit application in the City of Austin.\n\n## Sections\n\n1. **Project Information** (pp. 1-2)\n   Details the project type, name, street address, proposed development description, and legal deed references.\n   File: `01-project-information.md`\n2. **Contacts Information** (pp. 2-3)\n   Provides contact details for the applicant, owner, engineer, and other professionals involved in the project.\n   File: `02-contacts-information.md`\n3. **Property Attributes** (pp. 3-4)\n   Outlines jurisdictional boundaries, housing program participation, watershed details, and street/neighborhood plans for the site.\n   File: `03-property-attributes.md`\n4. **Application Assessment and Site Area** (p. 5)\n   Contains traffic impact analysis requirements, site area calculations, existing/proposed zoning use tables, and impervious cover percentages.\n   File: `04-application-assessment-and-site-area.md`\n5. **Land Use, Waivers, and Arborist Review** (p. 6)\n   Details compatibility standards, requested waivers or variances, and tree counts within and adjacent to the limits of construction.\n   File: `05-land-use-waivers-and-arborist-review.md`\n6. **Authorizations and Acknowledgments** (pp. 7-8)\n   Contains applicant signatures for submittal verification, inspection authorization, and general acknowledgments regarding property restrictions.\n   File: `06-authorizations-and-acknowledgments.md`\n","numLines":25,"startLine":1,"totalLines":25}}}
{"level":30,"time":1778273662689,"pid":56878,"hostname":"Mac.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01FWCvXKvuCUG15rkTirwr5t","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"These are just overviews. Let me check if I need to see the actual utilities section. Actually, the key issue is that the vision check clearly showed trees planted within 1-2 feet (or at) the overhead electric line, which is a major violation.\n\nNow I need to determine if those trees are utility-compatible or not. Based on the plant schedule, the trees being planted are:\n1. 2x Live Oak - mature height 40-60 feet (LARGE TREE)\n2. 10x Cedar Elm - mature height 40-50 feet (LARGE TREE)\n3. 2x Texas Mountain Laurel - mature height 20-25 feet (smaller)\n4. 6x Mexican Redbud - mature height 15-20 feet (smaller)\n5. Plus shrubs\n\nLive Oak and Cedar Elm are definitely NOT utility-compatible trees. They are large trees with extensive branch structures and root systems that are problematic for electric utilities. These are both major violations if they are within 15 feet of the distribution line.\n\nLet me now look at what species the vision tool identified as being planted near the OHE line. The vision tool mentioned:\n- \"Tree in the southwest corner\" - 1.2 ft from OHE\n- \"Westernmost tree of the cluster in the southeast corner\" - 72.4 ft from OHE  \n- \"Middle tree of the cluster in the southeast corner\" - 1.1 ft from OHE\n- \"Easternmost tree of the cluster in the southeast corner\" - 0 ft from OHE (AT the line)\n\nI need to determine what species these trees are. The landscape plan symbols should tell me. Let me look more carefully at the block 2 description from Sheet 31 which mentioned tree symbols.\n\nFrom the blocks.md of Sheet 31, I see the legend defines:\n- SY - STREET YARD TREE (EXCESS OF 1.5 CAL. INCH COUNTING TOWARDS MITIGATION)\n\n\n- PL - PARKING LOT TREE (EXCESS OF 1.5 CAL. INCH COUNTING TOWARDS MITIGATION)\n- P - PARKING BUFFER POINT TREE\n- M - MITIGATION TREE\n\nThe plant schedule shows shade trees (100 gal.) as Live Oak and Cedar Elm, while ornamental trees (45 gal.) are Texas Mountain Laurel and Mexican Redbud. The vision tool identified trees in the southwest and southeast corners, with the southeast corner being where the detention pond is located.\n\nThe key issue is determining which species are planted near the overhead electric line. Large, non-utility-compatible species like Live Oak and Cedar Elm planted within 15 feet would violate EL-2.1 and EL-2.3. I need to systematically check the critical distribution line requirements and identify the specific species in those close locations to assess compliance.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2051,"cache_read_input_tokens":54275,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2051},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"90431bd8-bd79-4eb9-bd46-3969546723e7","uuid":"0ab21c6d-1944-4c59-9dce-0f55be8623c8"}
