Water Flow Mitigation Rule (COA ECM 1.12.0 and COA Item No. 658S of the SSM) provision that all trenching greater than 5 feet deep must be inspected by a geologist (Texas P.G.) or a geologist's representative.\n    37→\n    38→## Block 7: notes — A block listing two benchmarks (B.M. #1 and B.M. #2) with descriptions indicating they are mag nails set in asphalt, along with their respective elevations in NAVD 88 (GEOID 18).\n    39→\n    40→**Benchmarks**\n    41→B.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    42→Elevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    43→\n    44→B.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    45→Elevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    46→\n    47→## Block 12: text_block — A block providing the legal description of the property, identifying it as Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision.\n    48→\n    49→**Legal Description**\n    50→Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n    51→\n    52→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"90431bd8-bd79-4eb9-bd46-3969546723e7","uuid":"f83086df-4105-42ab-8d92-5d24889f8a0f","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-21/blocks.md","content":"# Sheet 21 — Content Blocks\n\n## Block 2: diagram — A graphic scale bar showing distances of 0, 20, and 40 feet, along with the text 'Scale 1\" = 20''. A north arrow is also present above the scale bar.\n\n[Graphic scale bar showing distances 0, 20, 40]\nScale 1\" = 20'\n[North arrow pointing upwards and slightly to the right]\n\n## Block 3: drawing — This block contains a detailed site plan showing multiple buildings (Bldg. 1 to Bldg. 8) and their respective units, with FFE (Finished Floor Elevation) and square footage information. The plan includes electrical elements such as transformers, pull boxes, underground electric lines, and electrical meters. Additionally, the plan shows topography lines, property boundaries, and various easements like drainage, public utility, and wastewater. Information on adjoining properties is provided, detailing acreage, zoning, and use.\n\nA detailed site plan showing multiple buildings (Bldg. 1 to Bldg. 8) and their respective units, with FFE (Finished Floor Elevation) and square footage information. The plan includes electrical elements such as transformers, pull boxes, underground electric lines, and electrical meters. Additionally, the plan shows topography lines, property boundaries, and various easements like drainage, public utility, and wastewater. Information on adjoining properties is provided, detailing acreage, zoning, and use.\n\n## Block 4: notes — This block contains 'Utility Notes' detailing contractor responsibilities regarding existing utilities and specific requirements for private domestic water and wastewater branches. Below the notes are disclaimers about the application not verifying all data and the reliance on record information for existing utilities.\n\n**Utility Notes**\n1. Contractor shall verify horizontal and vertical location of existing utilities prior to construction.\n2. Contractor shall verify location of existing line, point of connection and notify engineer of any conflicts/discrepancies prior to installation of improvements.\n3. All Private Domestic Water Branches to the buildings are to be 2\" HDPE service tube per Detail 520-AW-01B\n4. All Private Wastewater Branches to the building are to be 6\" PVC service connections per Detail 520-AW-01C\n\nRelease of this application does not constitute a verification of all data, information and calculations supplied by the applicant. The engineer of record is solely responsible for the completeness, accuracy and adequacy of his/her submittal, whether or not the application is reviewed for Code compliance by City engineers.\n\nThe location of all existing utilities shown on these plans has been based upon record information only and may not match locations as constructed. The contractor shall contact Texas 811 for assistance in determining existing utility locations prior to beginning construction. Contractor shall field verify locations of utility crossings prior to beginning construction.\n\n## Block 5: notes — A small block containing text that references another sheet for profile information.\n\nSee Sht. 19\nfor Profile\n\n## Block 6: notes — A block containing a 'Tree List' reference stating 'Refer to landscape plan sheet 32 for the tree list.' Below it is a 'Note' regarding a Void and Water Flow Mitigation Rule that requires trenching over 5 feet deep to be inspected by a geologist.\n\n**Tree List**\nRefer to landscape plan sheet 32 for the tree list.\n\n**Note**\nThis project is subject to the Void and Water Flow Mitigation Rule (COA ECM 1.12.0 and COA Item No. 658S of the SSM) provision that all trenching greater than 5 feet deep must be inspected by a geologist (Texas P.G.) or a geologist's representative.\n\n## Block 7: notes — A block listing two benchmarks (B.M. #1 and B.M. #2) with descriptions indicating they are mag nails set in asphalt, along with their respective elevations in NAVD 88 (GEOID 18).\n\n**Benchmarks**\nB.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n\nB.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n\n## Block 12: text_block — A block providing the legal description of the property, identifying it as Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision.\n\n**Legal Description**\nLot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n\n","numLines":52,"startLine":1,"totalLines":52}}}
{"level":30,"time":1778273902821,"pid":56878,"hostname":"Mac.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01NdkCtT2eQanNkVEERBUhpW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good! Now I have clarity. Sheet 21 (Electrical Design Plan) shows UNDERGROUND electric lines, transformers, pull boxes, and meters. The guide for Sheet 31 mentioned that overhead electric (OHE) is shown on the landscape plan, but that's likely just referring to the presence of the overhead service in the area (shown as a utility line crossing the property or along the property boundary).\n\nSo the infrastructure I need to analyze is:\n1. OVERHEAD ELECTRIC line shown on landscape plan (Sheet 31) along the south property boundary (possibly the property line utility service from the street)\n2. UNDERGROUND electric lines shown on Sheet 21 (transformers, pull boxes within the project)\n\nThe vision measurements showed trees planted 0-2 feet from the OHE line. These are likely mitigation trees (Texas Mountain Laurel or Mexican Redbud based on the plant schedule) being planted in the landscape areas along the south property boundary.\n\nNow I need to determine if these are utility-compatible. Let me check the facts document to see if there's any detail about utility companies or coordination.\n\nActually, let me think about what I need to verify for each checklist item:\n\n**CRITICAL FINDINGS SO FAR:**\n1. Trees are planted 0-2 feet from an overhead electric line on the south/southeast boundary\n2. The trees are identified as \"Mitigation Trees\" (symbol M) per the landscape plan legend\n3. The likely species are Texas Mountain Laurel and Mexican Redbud (from the plant schedule)\n4. I need to verify if these are utility-compatible per ECM Appendix F\n5. I need to check for transmission lines (none apparent)\n6. I need to check for poles (vision results indicated southeast corner trees)\n7. I need to check for underground equipment locations\n\nThe grouping guide mentioned that:\n- \"Utility-compatible trees\": Trees marked with \"x\" in the \"UC\" column of ECM Appendix F; generally mature height ≤ 20 feet with some exceptions (e.g., Goldenball Lead-Tree at 25 ft, Texas Mountain Laurel at 25 ft)\n\n\n- Texas Mountain Laurel is specifically listed as utility-compatible, which suggests it's marked UC in the appendix. But the real issue here is the proximity—planting these trees essentially at the conductor line (0-2 feet away) is problematic regardless of species compatibility, as it could restrict maintenance access and violate clearance requirements. I need to determine if Austin Energy has approved this deviation as required by EL-2.15.\n\nThe applicability hinges on whether overhead distribution lines are actually present. Looking at the site details, there are dedicated utility easements (5' and 7.5') along the property boundaries, and the OHE line shown on the landscape plan along the south boundary appears to be existing utility infrastructure. This means the grouping does apply, and I should now work through each checklist requirement systematically.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1595,"cache_read_input_tokens":63087,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1595},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"90431bd8-bd79-4eb9-bd46-3969546723e7","uuid":"fe41dd01-0d6e-4c6a-beae-aecf0d83008c"}
