ns, curb details, and ADA compliance parameters. READ for exact symbol definitions and maximum allowable slopes.\n    28→*   **Block 3**: Benchmark specifications detailing the exact vertical control points (Mag Nail in Asphalt) for the site. READ for exact baseline elevation data.\n    29→*   **Block 5**: Curb grading note detailing conditions for catch curbs vs. spill curbs. READ for grading flow instructions. \n    30→*   **Block 6**: Tree list reference directing the user to landscape plan sheet 32. READ for sheet reference.\n    31→*   **Block 4**: Legal description of the subject property (Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision). READ to confirm property identity.\n    32→*   **Block 1**: Graphic scale bar. READ to confirm the 1\" = 20' measurement baseline.\n    33→*   **Blocks 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14**: Boilerplate & Administrative information, including the Texas 811 call warning, engineer's seal (Jason K. Rodgers), project metadata (Sheet 11 of 39, Project No. RJ3 70496), title block, and standard utility and design disclaimers. READ for project details or administrative tracking; otherwise, these can be skipped during a technical review.\n    34→\n    35→---\n    36→\n    37→All other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n    38→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"90431bd8-bd79-4eb9-bd46-3969546723e7","uuid":"405cc788-c03e-4304-80e8-b584cdebcf90","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-11/guide.md","content":"# Sheet 11 — Grading Plan\n\nThis sheet displays the detailed grading plan for the development site.\n\n## Reading Guide\n\nThis sheet presents the Grading Plan for the Valley View Townhomes multi-family development located at 3759 Valley View Road, Austin, Texas. Its primary purpose is to detail existing and proposed topography, establish building finished floor elevations (FFEs), map storm drainage infrastructure, and indicate the location of retaining walls and utility easements across the site.\n\n**Key values**:\n*   **Scale**: 1\" = 20'\n*   **Benchmark 1**: Elevation = 678.52' NAVD 88\n*   **Benchmark 2**: Elevation = 681.08' NAVD 88\n*   **Bldg 1**: 3,290 sf | FFE = 687.50\n*   **Bldg 2**: 4,830 sf | FFE = 685.50\n*   **Bldg 3**: 4,144 sf | FFE = 685.50\n*   **Bldg 4**: 4,144 sf | FFE = 685.50\n*   **Bldg 5**: 3,290 sf | FFE = 683.00\n*   **Bldg 6**: 3,290 sf | FFE = 683.00\n*   **Bldg 7**: 4,144 sf | FFE = 685.00\n*   **Bldg 8**: 4,997 sf | FFE = 685.50\n*   **ADA Ramp Maximum Slope**: 8.33%\n*   **Level Landing Maximum Slope**: 2%\n\n**Content blocks on this sheet**:\n\n*   **Block 2**: Main site grading drawing showing the layout of the 8 buildings, proposed/existing contours, retaining walls, storm drainage, and various utility easements. Use VISION to examine the spatial layout, specific contour line variations, spot elevations, and the exact placement of infrastructure and adjacent property boundaries.\n*   **Block 7**: Plan legend detailing line types and symbols for contours, limits of construction, storm drains, curb details, and ADA compliance parameters. READ for exact symbol definitions and maximum allowable slopes.\n*   **Block 3**: Benchmark specifications detailing the exact vertical control points (Mag Nail in Asphalt) for the site. READ for exact baseline elevation data.\n*   **Block 5**: Curb grading note detailing conditions for catch curbs vs. spill curbs. READ for grading flow instructions. \n*   **Block 6**: Tree list reference directing the user to landscape plan sheet 32. READ for sheet reference.\n*   **Block 4**: Legal description of the subject property (Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision). READ to confirm property identity.\n*   **Block 1**: Graphic scale bar. READ to confirm the 1\" = 20' measurement baseline.\n*   **Blocks 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14**: Boilerplate & Administrative information, including the Texas 811 call warning, engineer's seal (Jason K. Rodgers), project metadata (Sheet 11 of 39, Project No. RJ3 70496), title block, and standard utility and design disclaimers. READ for project details or administrative tracking; otherwise, these can be skipped during a technical review.\n\n---\n\nAll other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n","numLines":38,"startLine":1,"totalLines":38}}}
{"level":30,"time":1778273902821,"pid":56878,"hostname":"Mac.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01RnnQqhmCkzS6QDYFzxsWM6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The Grading Plan shows utility easements but doesn't specifically detail transmission easement boundaries (which would indicate if there are transmission lines requiring the 50-foot clearances). The facts document earlier mentioned various recorded easements but indicated there are no transmission easements noted.\n\nLet me now focus on evaluating the checklist items systematically based on what I've found:\n\n**KEY EVIDENCE GATHERED:**\n1. Overhead electric line (OHE) shown on landscape plan (Sheet 31) along south property boundary\n2. Trees planted 0-2 feet from the OHE line (vision measurements)\n3. Trees marked as \"Mitigation Trees\" (symbol M)\n4. Likely species: Texas Mountain Laurel (2 trees) and Mexican Redbud (6 trees)\n5. No transmission lines apparent on plans\n6. No transmission easements in facts.md\n7. The grouping guide specifically mentions Texas Mountain Laurel as utility-compatible\n8. Underground electric infrastructure on Sheet 21 (transformers, pull boxes)\n9. No Austin Energy approval documents apparent in the supplementary documents\n\n**APPLICABILITY CHECK:**\n- YES, this grouping applies because there ARE overhead electric distribution facilities (OHE line shown on landscape plan)\n\n**EVALUATION OF CHECKLIST ITEMS:**\n\nLet me now create a comprehensive analysis of each item. I'll evaluate based on what I've found and note where I cannot fully verify due to lack of ECM Appendix F data.\n\nEL-2.1: Non-utility-compatible trees within 15 ft of distribution\n- Trees at 0-2 ft from OHE are within this zone\n- Trees are likely TML/Mexican Redbud - claimed to be utility-compatible in grouping guide\n- STATUS: Cannot fully verify without seeing ECM Appendix F directly, but evidence suggests likely PASS if TML/MR are confirmed as UC\n\nEL-2.2: Non-utility-compatible trees within 50 ft of transmission\n\n\n- No transmission lines visible on the plans\n- STATUS: N/A\n\nEL-2.3: Large trees (40+ ft) within 25 ft of distribution\n- The mitigation trees (TML/MR) both mature under 25 feet, so they don't meet the size threshold\n- Larger shade species like Live Oak and Cedar Elm aren't positioned near the OHE\n- STATUS: PASS\n\nEL-2.4: Large trees within 50 ft of transmission\n- Transmission lines absent from site\n- STATUS: N/A\n\nEL-2.5: Trees within transmission easement\n- No transmission easement documented\n- STATUS: N/A\n\nE\n\n-2.6: Trees within 10 ft of poles\n- Pole locations need verification against the site plan\n- The vision assessment noted trees in the southeast corner area\n- STATUS: UNCLEAR\n\nEL-2.7: Trees within 10 ft of pad-mounted equipment\n- Transformers are located on Sheet 21, but I need to measure the actual distances between them and the landscape plantings to determine compliance\n- STATUS: UNCLEAR\n\nEL-2.8: Trees within 5 ft of underground equipment\n- Pull boxes and transformers shown on Sheet 21 require distance verification\n- STATUS: UNCLEAR\n\nEL-2.9: Root barriers for trees within 20 ft of underground equipment\n- The plant schedule and landscape notes don't reference any root barrier specifications\n- STATUS: UNCLEAR\n\nEL-2.10: Plant legend/schedule completeness\n- Sheet 35 contains the plant schedule and legend with all species information in both common and botanical names\n- STATUS: PASS\n\nEL-2.11: Large trees differentiation\n- The schedule distinguishes between 100 gallon shade trees and 45 gallon ornamental trees, though I should verify the mature height specifications\n- STATUS: LIKELY PASS\n\nEL-2.12: Measurements from conductor vs. pole\n- The measurements appear to be taken from the overhead conductor line, which is the correct approach\n- STATUS: PASS\n\nEL-2.13: Measurements from trunk vs. canopy\n- Distances were measured from the tree symbol locations rather than projected canopy spread, which is appropriate\n- STATUS: PASS\n\nEL-2.14: Trees shown in relation to overhead facilities\n- The landscape plan clearly shows tree locations relative to the overhead electrical line\n- STATUS: PASS\n\nEL-2.15: Austin Energy approval for trees in restricted zones\n- Trees positioned 0-2 feet from the OHE fall within the restricted zone (less than 15 feet)\n- I haven't located any Austin Energy approval documentation in the submitted materials\n- The key uncertainty is whether Texas Mountain Laurel and Mexican Redbud qualify as utility-compatible species under ECM Appendix F, though the grouping guide does reference Texas Mountain Laurel as an example of a utility-compatible option\n- STATUS: FAIL or UNVERIFIABLE\n\nI need to do another visual check of the plan to pinpoint transformer locations and their relationship to the proposed plantings.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1917,"cache_read_input_tokens":64682,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1917},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"90431bd8-bd79-4eb9-bd46-3969546723e7","uuid":"456ab7d9-101c-4aba-98c9-5354e74ee9e9"}
