{"level":30,"time":1778274478325,"pid":56878,"hostname":"Mac.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":6,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011mNeha1P7mZcj9LSCb111q","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 17 — Conveyance Cross Sections\n     2→\n     3→Conveyance cross-section graphs and corresponding site plan view.\n     4→\n     5→## Reading Guide\n     6→\n     7→This sheet contains the conveyance cross-sections and a corresponding site plan snippet for the Valley View Townhomes project. Its primary purpose is to model and display the hydraulic performance (water surface elevations, velocities, and channel depths) of the site's drainage conveyance system during 25-year and 100-year storm events at three distinct cross-section locations (XS 1, XS 2, and XS 3).\n     8→\n     9→**Key values:**\n    10→*   **Manning's Roughness Coefficient (n):** 0.03 used for all cross-sections\n    11→*   **Design Flow Rates (Q):** 16.49 cfs (25-Year event), 21.97 cfs (100-Year event) across all sections\n    12→*   **Maximum 100-Year Velocity:** 8.70 ft/s (at cross-section XS 3)\n    13→*   **Maximum 100-Year Depth:** 0.48 ft (at cross-section XS 3)\n    14→*   **Benchmark 1:** Mag Nail in Asphalt, Elevation = 678.52' NAVD 88\n    15→*   **Benchmark 2:** Mag Nail in Asphalt, Elevation = 681.08' NAVD 88\n    16→*   **Finished Floor Elevations (FFE):** Bldg. 5 = 683.0 ft, Bldg. 6 = 683.0 ft\n    17→\n    18→**Content blocks on this sheet:**\n    19→\n    20→*   **Block 7: site plan — Multi-family development plan view**\n    21→    Use VISION to examine the spatial layout, grading contours, drainage easements, and the specific locations of the cross-section cut lines (XS 1, XS 2, XS 3) relative to buildings 5 and 6 and existing retaining walls.\n    22→*   **Blocks 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6: diagrams — Cross-section profile graphs**\n    23→    Use VISION to examine the channel profiles, side slopes, and plotted water surface elevations for cross-sections XS 1, XS 2, and XS 3 under both 25-year and 100-year storm conditions. \n    24→*   **Blocks 14, 15, 16, 17, 19, 20: tables — Hydraulic data tables**\n    25→    READ for exact hydraulic parameters corresponding to each cross-section graph, including Depth (ft), Flow Rate Q (cfs), Area (sqft), Velocity (ft/s), Wetted Perimeter Wp (ft), Critical Depth Yc (ft), Top Width (ft), and Energy Head (ft).\n    26→*   **Block 21: text_block — Hydraulic modeling note**\n    27→    READ to verify the Manning's roughness coefficient used in the calculations.\n    28→*   **Block 12: notes — Benchmarks**\n    29→    READ for exact elevation values and physical descriptions of the survey benchmarks (B.M. #1 and B.M. #2).\n    30→*   **Block 8: notes — Legend**\n    31→    READ for definitions of line types and symbols used in the plan view, such as existing/proposed contours, storm drain lines, and limits of construction.\n    32→*   **Block 9: notes — Construction and landscape notes**\n    33→    READ for curb construction grading instructions (catch curb vs. spill curb) and references to the tree list.\n    34→*   **Blocks 10, 11, 13, 18: various — Boilerplate, Title Block & Administrative**\n    35→    READ for the legal description of the property, project title (Valley View Townhomes), engineer seal (Bleyl Engineering), standard utility/Texas 811 warnings, and city submittal disclaimers.\n    36→\n    37→---\n    38→\n    39→All other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n    40→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ad5307f2-d9b7-488b-8d54-395eaf0212d6","uuid":"3d14767c-0934-4298-89df-5452751551c3","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-17/guide.md","content":"# Sheet 17 — Conveyance Cross Sections\n\nConveyance cross-section graphs and corresponding site plan view.\n\n## Reading Guide\n\nThis sheet contains the conveyance cross-sections and a corresponding site plan snippet for the Valley View Townhomes project. Its primary purpose is to model and display the hydraulic performance (water surface elevations, velocities, and channel depths) of the site's drainage conveyance system during 25-year and 100-year storm events at three distinct cross-section locations (XS 1, XS 2, and XS 3).\n\n**Key values:**\n*   **Manning's Roughness Coefficient (n):** 0.03 used for all cross-sections\n*   **Design Flow Rates (Q):** 16.49 cfs (25-Year event), 21.97 cfs (100-Year event) across all sections\n*   **Maximum 100-Year Velocity:** 8.70 ft/s (at cross-section XS 3)\n*   **Maximum 100-Year Depth:** 0.48 ft (at cross-section XS 3)\n*   **Benchmark 1:** Mag Nail in Asphalt, Elevation = 678.52' NAVD 88\n*   **Benchmark 2:** Mag Nail in Asphalt, Elevation = 681.08' NAVD 88\n*   **Finished Floor Elevations (FFE):** Bldg. 5 = 683.0 ft, Bldg. 6 = 683.0 ft\n\n**Content blocks on this sheet:**\n\n*   **Block 7: site plan — Multi-family development plan view**\n    Use VISION to examine the spatial layout, grading contours, drainage easements, and the specific locations of the cross-section cut lines (XS 1, XS 2, XS 3) relative to buildings 5 and 6 and existing retaining walls.\n*   **Blocks 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6: diagrams — Cross-section profile graphs**\n    Use VISION to examine the channel profiles, side slopes, and plotted water surface elevations for cross-sections XS 1, XS 2, and XS 3 under both 25-year and 100-year storm conditions. \n*   **Blocks 14, 15, 16, 17, 19, 20: tables — Hydraulic data tables**\n    READ for exact hydraulic parameters corresponding to each cross-section graph, including Depth (ft), Flow Rate Q (cfs), Area (sqft), Velocity (ft/s), Wetted Perimeter Wp (ft), Critical Depth Yc (ft), Top Width (ft), and Energy Head (ft).\n*   **Block 21: text_block — Hydraulic modeling note**\n    READ to verify the Manning's roughness coefficient used in the calculations.\n*   **Block 12: notes — Benchmarks**\n    READ for exact elevation values and physical descriptions of the survey benchmarks (B.M. #1 and B.M. #2).\n*   **Block 8: notes — Legend**\n    READ for definitions of line types and symbols used in the plan view, such as existing/proposed contours, storm drain lines, and limits of construction.\n*   **Block 9: notes — Construction and landscape notes**\n    READ for curb construction grading instructions (catch curb vs. spill curb) and references to the tree list.\n*   **Blocks 10, 11, 13, 18: various — Boilerplate, Title Block & Administrative**\n    READ for the legal description of the property, project title (Valley View Townhomes), engineer seal (Bleyl Engineering), standard utility/Texas 811 warnings, and city submittal disclaimers.\n\n---\n\nAll other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n","numLines":40,"startLine":1,"totalLines":40}}}
