{"level":30,"time":1778274502421,"pid":56878,"hostname":"Mac.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HKQyQ4YW5xkdeeMbUTBZ9b","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 3 — General Notes\n     2→\n     3→Comprehensive general notes, sequence of construction, and project legend.\n     4→\n     5→## Reading Guide\n     6→\n     7→This sheet provides a comprehensive collection of general notes, standard specifications, utility capacity approvals, and the site's legend for the Valley View Townhomes project. Its primary purpose is to establish project-wide regulatory constraints, outline the construction sequence, and document the City of Austin's confirmation of sufficient water and wastewater service.\n     8→\n     9→**Key values:**\n    10→*   Project Scope: 39 Condo Units, 28 Living Unit Equivalents (LUE), 3.59 Acres\n    11→*   Water Demand: 60 gpm (Estimated Peak Hour), requiring connection to existing 8-inch main\n    12→*   Fire Flow Requirement: 1,500 gpm\n    13→*   Wastewater Flow: 20 gpm (Estimated Peak Wet Weather), requiring connection to existing 8-inch gravity main\n    14→*   Benchmark 1 (Mag Nail in Asphalt): Elevation = 678.52' NAVD 88\n    15→*   Benchmark 2 (Mag Nail in Asphalt): Elevation = 681.08' NAVD 88\n    16→*   Fire Department Paving Requirement: 80,000 lb. live-vehicle load capability\n    17→*   Fire Apparatus Vertical Clearance: 14 feet\n    18→*   Fire Hydrant Placement: Center of 4-inch opening must be at least 18 inches above finished grade\n    19→*   Electric Transmission Clearances: No digging/grading/trees within 25 feet of transmission structures; 20-foot clearance for aerial equipment/staging\n    20→*   Transmission Easement Road Requirement: Must support a 46,000 lb. tandem axle\n    21→*   ROW Work Zone Peak Hours: 6:00 a.m. to 9:00 a.m. and 4:00 p.m. to 6:00 p.m.\n    22→*   ROW Violation Fee: $250 per specific violation type\n    23→\n    24→**Content blocks on this sheet:**\n    25→\n    26→*   **Blocks 12 & 15:** Austin Water \"Suitable and Sufficient Existing Service\" approval letters for water and wastewater. READ the block content for the approved project specifications, demand calculations, and the specific existing 8-inch mains designated for the tap.\n    27→*   **Block 17:** Benchmark data. READ for exact elevations and locations to be used as control points for the site.\n    28→*   **Block 19:** Sequence of Construction. READ the block content for the strict 13-step chronological order of operations, particularly regarding when erosion controls and pond grading must occur.\n    29→*   **Block 6:** Project Legend. READ for the text descriptions of utilities, and use VISION to familiarize yourself with the symbol shapes (e.g., property lines, utility lines, tree protection zones) to interpret subsequent plan sheets accurately.\n    30→*   **Block 9:** Fire Department requirements. READ for strict design constraints regarding emergency access, pavement load ratings, and fire hydrant placement.\n    31→*   **Block 16:** Electric Transmission Notes. READ for critical safety clearances, structural load requirements for roads in easements, and barricade rules required by Austin Energy.\n    32→*   **Block 7:** Right-of-Way Management Standard Notes. READ for traffic control rules, restricted working hours, and permit violation penalties.\n    33→*   **Block 8:** General Construction Notes and Developer Information. READ to identify the property owner (WM 41 Austin RE LLC) and general engineering responsibilities.\n    34→*   **Blocks 14 & 18:** Electric Notes and Austin Energy standard requirements. READ for tree pruning, easement access, and code compliance constraints.\n    35→*   **Block 11:** Site Plan Release Notes. READ for general City of Austin demolition and utility relocation policies. \n    36→*   **Block 20:** Legal Description. READ to verify the exact lot and subdivision references (Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision).\n    37→*   **Blocks 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 10, 13:** Various administrative and boilerplate content (Title block info, Engineer's seal, Texas 811 logo, ADA disclaimers). READ the block content briefly for project tracking metadata, otherwise these can be safely skipped.\n    38→\n    39→---\n    40→\n    41→- `block-6.md` — table: Legend for existing utility and feature symbols used on the site plan.\n    42→- `block-9.md` — text_block: Standard notes for managing construction within the Right-Of-Way, covering violations, permitting, t\n    43→- `block-10.md` — text_block: List of 13 steps detailing the construction sequence, from installing temporary erosion controls to \n    44→- `block-13.md` — text_block: List of 16 notes detailing restrictions and requirements for work near electric transmission lines a\n    45→- `block-14.md` — text_block: Copy of a letter from Austin Water confirming that the existing wastewater main has adequate capacit\n    46→- `block-16.md` — text_block: Copy of a letter from Austin Water confirming that the existing water main has adequate capacity for\n    47→- `block-17.md` — text_block: Additional notes outlining requirements from Austin Energy regarding easements, tree work, erosion c\n    48→- `block-19.md` — text_block: List of 5 notes regarding electric utilities, specifically referencing Austin Energy's requirements \n    49→\n    50→All other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n    51→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d95a2e27-fdb9-4bac-a4d1-7990111a8624","uuid":"de32f8d8-cffe-443e-b20d-ad91f6a3cd41","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-03/guide.md","content":"# Sheet 3 — General Notes\n\nComprehensive general notes, sequence of construction, and project legend.\n\n## Reading Guide\n\nThis sheet provides a comprehensive collection of general notes, standard specifications, utility capacity approvals, and the site's legend for the Valley View Townhomes project. Its primary purpose is to establish project-wide regulatory constraints, outline the construction sequence, and document the City of Austin's confirmation of sufficient water and wastewater service.\n\n**Key values:**\n*   Project Scope: 39 Condo Units, 28 Living Unit Equivalents (LUE), 3.59 Acres\n*   Water Demand: 60 gpm (Estimated Peak Hour), requiring connection to existing 8-inch main\n*   Fire Flow Requirement: 1,500 gpm\n*   Wastewater Flow: 20 gpm (Estimated Peak Wet Weather), requiring connection to existing 8-inch gravity main\n*   Benchmark 1 (Mag Nail in Asphalt): Elevation = 678.52' NAVD 88\n*   Benchmark 2 (Mag Nail in Asphalt): Elevation = 681.08' NAVD 88\n*   Fire Department Paving Requirement: 80,000 lb. live-vehicle load capability\n*   Fire Apparatus Vertical Clearance: 14 feet\n*   Fire Hydrant Placement: Center of 4-inch opening must be at least 18 inches above finished grade\n*   Electric Transmission Clearances: No digging/grading/trees within 25 feet of transmission structures; 20-foot clearance for aerial equipment/staging\n*   Transmission Easement Road Requirement: Must support a 46,000 lb. tandem axle\n*   ROW Work Zone Peak Hours: 6:00 a.m. to 9:00 a.m. and 4:00 p.m. to 6:00 p.m.\n*   ROW Violation Fee: $250 per specific violation type\n\n**Content blocks on this sheet:**\n\n*   **Blocks 12 & 15:** Austin Water \"Suitable and Sufficient Existing Service\" approval letters for water and wastewater. READ the block content for the approved project specifications, demand calculations, and the specific existing 8-inch mains designated for the tap.\n*   **Block 17:** Benchmark data. READ for exact elevations and locations to be used as control points for the site.\n*   **Block 19:** Sequence of Construction. READ the block content for the strict 13-step chronological order of operations, particularly regarding when erosion controls and pond grading must occur.\n*   **Block 6:** Project Legend. READ for the text descriptions of utilities, and use VISION to familiarize yourself with the symbol shapes (e.g., property lines, utility lines, tree protection zones) to interpret subsequent plan sheets accurately.\n*   **Block 9:** Fire Department requirements. READ for strict design constraints regarding emergency access, pavement load ratings, and fire hydrant placement.\n*   **Block 16:** Electric Transmission Notes. READ for critical safety clearances, structural load requirements for roads in easements, and barricade rules required by Austin Energy.\n*   **Block 7:** Right-of-Way Management Standard Notes. READ for traffic control rules, restricted working hours, and permit violation penalties.\n*   **Block 8:** General Construction Notes and Developer Information. READ to identify the property owner (WM 41 Austin RE LLC) and general engineering responsibilities.\n*   **Blocks 14 & 18:** Electric Notes and Austin Energy standard requirements. READ for tree pruning, easement access, and code compliance constraints.\n*   **Block 11:** Site Plan Release Notes. READ for general City of Austin demolition and utility relocation policies. \n*   **Block 20:** Legal Description. READ to verify the exact lot and subdivision references (Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision).\n*   **Blocks 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 10, 13:** Various administrative and boilerplate content (Title block info, Engineer's seal, Texas 811 logo, ADA disclaimers). READ the block content briefly for project tracking metadata, otherwise these can be safely skipped.\n\n---\n\n- `block-6.md` — table: Legend for existing utility and feature symbols used on the site plan.\n- `block-9.md` — text_block: Standard notes for managing construction within the Right-Of-Way, covering violations, permitting, t\n- `block-10.md` — text_block: List of 13 steps detailing the construction sequence, from installing temporary erosion controls to \n- `block-13.md` — text_block: List of 16 notes detailing restrictions and requirements for work near electric transmission lines a\n- `block-14.md` — text_block: Copy of a letter from Austin Water confirming that the existing wastewater main has adequate capacit\n- `block-16.md` — text_block: Copy of a letter from Austin Water confirming that the existing water main has adequate capacity for\n- `block-17.md` — text_block: Additional notes outlining requirements from Austin Energy regarding easements, tree work, erosion c\n- `block-19.md` — text_block: List of 5 notes regarding electric utilities, specifically referencing Austin Energy's requirements \n\nAll other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n","numLines":51,"startLine":1,"totalLines":51}}}
{"level":30,"time":1778274502421,"pid":56878,"hostname":"Mac.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011sGdciNxSMsRUjSkfNca8L","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016qrLhQEkEeCF7nRN8NaHLK","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-03/blocks.md","limit":200},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3740,"cache_read_input_tokens":57577,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3740},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d95a2e27-fdb9-4bac-a4d1-7990111a8624","uuid":"0d966c40-eb6b-4246-825e-c9bc7fd36a32"}
{"level":30,"time":1778274502421,"pid":56878,"hostname":"Mac.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016qrLhQEkEeCF7nRN8NaHLK","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 3 — Content Blocks\n     2→\n     3→## Block 7: text_block — Notice stating the applicant's responsibility for determining ADA compliance.\n     4→\n     5→Americans with Disabilities Act\n     6→The City of Austin has reviewed this plans for compliance with City development regulations only. The applicant, property owner, and occupant of the premises are responsible for determining whether the plan complies with all other laws, regulations , and restrictions which may be applicable to the property and its use.\n     7→\n     8→## Block 11: text_block — Legal description of the property for the site plan.\n     9→\n    10→Legal Description\n    11→Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n    12→\n    13→## Block 12: text_block — List of 10 site plan release notes outlining requirements and conditions for the approved site plan, including building codes, relocation costs, demolition permits, and utility service providers.\n    14→\n    15→Site Plan Release Notes\n    16→1. All improvements shall be made in accordance with the released site plan. Any additional improvements will require a site plan amendment and approval from the Development Services Department.\n    17→2. Approval of this site plan does not include Building Code and Fire Code approval; or building, demolition, or relocation permits approval. A City demolition or relocation permit can only be issued once the historic review process is completed.\n    18→3. All signs must comply with the requirements of the Land Development Code.(Chapter 25-10)\n    19→4. The owner is responsible for all costs of relocation of, or damage to, utilities.\n    20→5. Additional electric easements may be required at a later date.\n    21→6. A Site Development Permit must be issued prior to an application for building permit for non-consolidated or Land Use Commission approved site plans.\n    22→8. All existing structures shown to be removed will require a demolition permit from the City of Austin Development Services Department.\n    23→9. For construction within the right-of-way, a ROW excavation permit is required.\n    24→10. Water and wastewater service will be provided by the City of Austin (or identify the service provider if other than the City of Austin).\n    25→\n    26→## Block 15: text_block — List of 7 requirements from the Austin Fire Department regarding pavement, hydrants, installation timing, paving standards, dumpster placement, fire lanes, and vertical clearances.\n    27→\n    28→Fire Department\n    29→1. The Austin Fire Department requires asphalt or concrete pavement prior to construction as an \"all-weather driving surface.\"\n    30→2. Hydrants must be installed with the center of the four-inch opening at least 18 inches above finished grade. The four-inch opening must face the driveway or street with three- to six-foot setbacks from the curbline(s). No obstruction is allowed within three feet of any hydrant and the four-inch opening must be totally unobstructed from the street.\n    31→3. TIMING OF INSTALLATION: When fire protection facilities are installed by the developer, such facilities shall include all surface access roads which shall be installed and made serviceable prior to and during the time of construction. Where alternative methods of protection, as approved by the Fire Chief, are provided, the above may be modified or waived.\n    32→4. All pervious/decorative paving shall be engineered and installed for 80,000 lb. live-vehicle loads. Any pervious/decorative paving within 100 feet of any building must be approved by the Fire Department.\n    33→5. Commercial dumpsters and containers with an individual capacity of 1.5 cubic yards or greater shall not be stored or placed within ten feet of openings, combustible walls, or combustible eave lines.\n    34→6. Fire lanes designated on site plan shall be registered with City of Austin Fire Marshal's office and inspected for final approval.\n    35→7. Vertical clearance required for fire apparatus is 14 feet for full width of access drive.\n    36→\n    37→## Block 18: text_block — Locations and elevations for two benchmarks used in the project.\n    38→\n    39→Benchmarks\n    40→B.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    41→Elevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    42→\n    43→B.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    44→Elevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    45→\n    46→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d95a2e27-fdb9-4bac-a4d1-7990111a8624","uuid":"003b020c-dfdf-472b-b221-0f6f535863f9","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-03/blocks.md","content":"# Sheet 3 — Content Blocks\n\n## Block 7: text_block — Notice stating the applicant's responsibility for determining ADA compliance.\n\nAmericans with Disabilities Act\nThe City of Austin has reviewed this plans for compliance with City development regulations only. The applicant, property owner, and occupant of the premises are responsible for determining whether the plan complies with all other laws, regulations , and restrictions which may be applicable to the property and its use.\n\n## Block 11: text_block — Legal description of the property for the site plan.\n\nLegal Description\nLot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n\n## Block 12: text_block — List of 10 site plan release notes outlining requirements and conditions for the approved site plan, including building codes, relocation costs, demolition permits, and utility service providers.\n\nSite Plan Release Notes\n1. All improvements shall be made in accordance with the released site plan. Any additional improvements will require a site plan amendment and approval from the Development Services Department.\n2. Approval of this site plan does not include Building Code and Fire Code approval; or building, demolition, or relocation permits approval. A City demolition or relocation permit can only be issued once the historic review process is completed.\n3. All signs must comply with the requirements of the Land Development Code.(Chapter 25-10)\n4. The owner is responsible for all costs of relocation of, or damage to, utilities.\n5. Additional electric easements may be required at a later date.\n6. A Site Development Permit must be issued prior to an application for building permit for non-consolidated or Land Use Commission approved site plans.\n8. All existing structures shown to be removed will require a demolition permit from the City of Austin Development Services Department.\n9. For construction within the right-of-way, a ROW excavation permit is required.\n10. Water and wastewater service will be provided by the City of Austin (or identify the service provider if other than the City of Austin).\n\n## Block 15: text_block — List of 7 requirements from the Austin Fire Department regarding pavement, hydrants, installation timing, paving standards, dumpster placement, fire lanes, and vertical clearances.\n\nFire Department\n1. The Austin Fire Department requires asphalt or concrete pavement prior to construction as an \"all-weather driving surface.\"\n2. Hydrants must be installed with the center of the four-inch opening at least 18 inches above finished grade. The four-inch opening must face the driveway or street with three- to six-foot setbacks from the curbline(s). No obstruction is allowed within three feet of any hydrant and the four-inch opening must be totally unobstructed from the street.\n3. TIMING OF INSTALLATION: When fire protection facilities are installed by the developer, such facilities shall include all surface access roads which shall be installed and made serviceable prior to and during the time of construction. Where alternative methods of protection, as approved by the Fire Chief, are provided, the above may be modified or waived.\n4. All pervious/decorative paving shall be engineered and installed for 80,000 lb. live-vehicle loads. Any pervious/decorative paving within 100 feet of any building must be approved by the Fire Department.\n5. Commercial dumpsters and containers with an individual capacity of 1.5 cubic yards or greater shall not be stored or placed within ten feet of openings, combustible walls, or combustible eave lines.\n6. Fire lanes designated on site plan shall be registered with City of Austin Fire Marshal's office and inspected for final approval.\n7. Vertical clearance required for fire apparatus is 14 feet for full width of access drive.\n\n## Block 18: text_block — Locations and elevations for two benchmarks used in the project.\n\nBenchmarks\nB.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n\nB.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n\n","numLines":46,"startLine":1,"totalLines":46}}}
{"level":30,"time":1778274502421,"pid":56878,"hostname":"Mac.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":7,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01QodUeB7rjrRwviUeYAHZwV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is another critical finding! There are NO dimension lines or clearance measurements shown on the landscape plan for the trees relative to the overhead electric line.\n\nThis means:\n1. There's no way to verify the clearances from the landscape plan\n2. The clearances cannot be checked for compliance with the 15-foot, 25-foot, and 50-foot thresholds\n3. This is a deficiency: lack of required documentation\n\nLet me now check what the grouping guide says about the applicability. Looking back at the grouping description, it says:\n\n\"Applicability: Only when overhead electric facilities are present or proposed. Check: Site plan for distribution facility locations\"\n\nWe have confirmed:\n- Overhead electric lines ARE shown on the landscape plan (along south property line)\n- Trees ARE proposed near these lines (within 0.1 to 3.2 feet)\n- BUT the species are NOT identified on the landscape plan\n- AND no clearances are dimensioned\n\nThis grouping IS APPLICABLE. Now I need to evaluate each checklist item.\n\nLet me also check the specifications and utility notes on the electrical plan to see if it provides any utility compatibility information.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":626,"cache_read_input_tokens":55553,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":626},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4e1eed96-d749-4c9f-b941-3ae8c1ee87e2","uuid":"c2a0aa63-0efc-4cda-8e36-d5e169303297"}
{"level":30,"time":1778274502421,"pid":56878,"hostname":"Mac.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":7,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01QodUeB7rjrRwviUeYAHZwV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LnDFsidxCyuJUzbvmdXaz9","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-09/blocks.md","limit":150},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":626,"cache_read_input_tokens":55553,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":626},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4e1eed96-d749-4c9f-b941-3ae8c1ee87e2","uuid":"b2961395-5ef1-4485-9a26-4c3d4b28839c"}
{"level":30,"time":1778274502421,"pid":56878,"hostname":"Mac.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":7,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LnDFsidxCyuJUzbvmdXaz9","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 9 — Content Blocks\n     2→\n     3→## Block 1: drawing — Notes detailing lighting requirements, specifically that all exterior lighting must be full cut-off and fully shielded in compliance with Subchapter E 2.5. It also includes illustrations of fully-shielded light fixtures.\n     4→\n     5→Lighting Note\n     6→All exterior lighting will be full cut-off and fully shielded in compliance with Subchapter E 2.5. All site lighting to be located on the building will be in compliance with Subchapter E 2.5, and will be reviewed during building plan review. Any change or substitution of lams/light fixtures shall be submitted to the Director for approval in accordance with Section 2.5.2.E. See Figure 34.\n     7→[Illustration of four different fully-shielded light fixtures]\n     8→Figure 34\n     9→Examples of Fully-Shielded Light Fixtures\n    10→\n    11→## Block 2: drawing — This block contains a detailed site plan for the Valley View Townhomes project. It shows the layout of multiple buildings, driveways, sidewalks, parking spaces, detention pond, retaining walls, utility lines, and easements. Key features include buildings numbered 1 through 8, various unit numbers, concrete sidewalks, decomposed granite walkways, a mail kiosk, and existing structures to be replaced or retained.\n    12→\n    13→Site Plan showing layout of buildings (Bldg. 1 to Bldg. 8), driveways, parking, detention pond, utility lines, easements, existing trees, retaining walls, and surrounding properties.\n    14→\n    15→## Block 3: notes — A note specifying the required methods for installing fence post holes near a tree's Critical Root Zone (CRZ) to avoid damage, including manual digging and the application of hardwood mulch.\n    16→\n    17→Fence Note\n    18→Post holes that are to be installed near a trees Critical Root Zone (CRZ) will be dug by hand. No machinery is allowed in the CRZ and any work done in this area shall be done by hand. Add a 3-inch layer of hardwood mulch in the CRZ prior to commencing construction activities.\n    19→\n    20→## Block 4: notes — Notes regarding accessibility standards and requirements for the project, covering topics such as accessible parking, route cross-slopes, and ramp design.\n    21→\n    22→Accessibility Notes\n    23→1. Approval of these plans by the City of Austin indicates compliance with applicable City regulations only. Compliance with accessibility standards such as the 2010 Standards for Accessible Design or the 2012 Texas Accessibility Standards was not verified. The applicant is responsible for compliance with all applicable accessibility standards.\n    24→2. Accessible parking spaces must be located on a surface with a slope not exceeding 1:50.\n    25→3. Accessible routes must have a cross-slope no greater than 1:50.\n    26→4. Slopes on accessible routes may not exceed 1:20 unless designed as a ramp.\n    27→5. The maximum slope of a ramp in new construction is 1:12. The maximum rise for any ramp is 20in. The maximum horizontal projection is 30 feet for a ramp with a slope between 1:12 and 1:15, and 40 feet for a ramp with a slope between 1:16 and 1:20.\n    28→\n    29→## Block 5: notes — A list of notes related to the release of the site plan, covering topics such as compliance with the approved plan, necessary permits, signage, easements, utility service, demolition, condominium regulations, driveway construction, and right-of-way excavation.\n    30→\n    31→a. All improvements shall be made in accordance with the released site plan. Any additional improvements will require site plan amendment and approval of the Development Services Department.\n    32→b. Approval of this Site Plan does not include Building and Fire Code approval nor building permit approval.\n    33→c. All signs must comply with requirements of the Land Development Code (Chapter 25-10).\n    34→d. Additional electric easements may be required at a later date.\n    35→e. Water and wastewater service will be provided by the City of Austin (or identify the service provider if other than the City of Austin).\n    36→f. All existing structures shown to be removed will require a demolition permit from the City of Austin Development Services Department.\n    37→g. No certificate of occupancy may be issued for the proposed residential condominium project until the owner or owners of the property have complied with Chapter 81 and 82 of the Property Code of the State of Texas or any other statutes enacted by the State concerning condominiums.\n    38→h. For driveway construction: The owner is responsible for all costs for relocation of, or damage to utilities.\n    39→i. For construction within the right-of-way, a ROW excavation permit is required.\n    40→\n    41→## Block 6: notes — General notes outlining the engineer's responsibilities, the limitations of the provided utility locations, and the contractor's obligation to verify these locations prior to construction.\n    42→\n    43→Release of this application does not constitute a verification of all data, information and calculations supplied by the applicant. The engineer of record is solely responsible for the completeness, accuracy and adequacy of his/her submittal, whether or not the application is reviewed for Code compliance by City engineers.\n    44→The location of all existing utilities shown on these plans has been based upon record information only and may not match locations as constructed. The contractor shall contact Texas 811 for assistance in determining existing utility locations prior to beginning construction. Contractor shall field verify locations of utility crossings prior to beginning construction.\n    45→\n    46→## Block 7: notes — Notes detailing regulations for trash collection on the property, including rules against leaving containers at the curbside unattended and the requirement for visible signage.\n    47→\n    48→Trash Collection Notes\n    49→Trash containers shall not be left at curb side unattended. Signs must be posted on the building or at a location that is publicly visible indicating that trash containers may not be left at curbside. The sign shall indicate the City of Austin's Code enforcement phone number for the public to report violations.\n    50→A restrictive covenant may be required for enforcement purposes.\n    51→Reference: TCM, 9.4.2\n    52→\n    53→## Block 8: notes — Information about two benchmarks used for the project, including their location (Mag Nail Set in Asphalt) and elevation in NAVD 88 (GEOID 18).\n    54→\n    55→Benchmarks\n    56→B.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    57→Elevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    58→B.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    59→Elevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    60→\n    61→## Block 9: notes — The legal description of the property, identifying it as Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision, Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n    62→\n    63→Legal Description\n    64→Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n    65→\n    66→## Block 10: notes — A table detailing the parking requirements and provisions for the project, including land use, parking spaces provided (surface/garage), compact spaces, ADA spaces, and bicycle spaces.\n    67→\n    68→Parking Data\n    69→Land Use Residential\n    70→Parking Spaces Provided 10 Surface/ 78 Garage\n    71→Compact Spaces Provided 0\n    72→ADA Spaces Required X\n    73→ADA Spaces Provided X\n    74→Total Bicycle Spaces Required 5\n    75→Long Term Bicycle Spaces Required 3\n    76→Long Term Bicycle Spaces Provided 39 (1 per Garage)\n    77→Short Term Bicycle Spaces Required 2\n    78→Short Term Bicycle Spaces Provided 2\n    79→\n    80→## Block 11: other — A graphic scale indicating that 1 inch equals 20 feet on the site plan.\n    81→\n    82→0 20' 40'\n    83→Scale 1\" = 20'\n    84→\n    85→## Block 14: table — This table provides data on building heights, including the number of units, building type, average mean sea level (MSL), building height in feet, average building height (MSL), height allowed in feet, and height allowed (MSL).\n    86→\n    87→| Building # | Building Type | Average (MSL) | Building Height (FT) | Avg. Building Height (MSL) | Height Allowed (FT) | Height Allowed (MSL) |\n    88→| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n    89→| 1 | 4 Plex | 686.25 | 35' - 10.75\" | 722.15 | 40' | 726.25 |\n    90→| 2 | 6 Plex | 684.50 | 33' - 4.75\" | 717.90 | 40' | 724.50 |\n    91→| 3 | 5 Plex | 685.00 | 36' - 1\" | 721.08 | 40' | 725.00 |\n    92→| 4 | 5 Plex | 684.25 | 33' - 4.75\" | 717.65 | 40' | 724.25 |\n    93→| 5 | 4 Plex | 682.17 | 35' - 10.75\" | 718.07 | 40' | 722.17 |\n    94→| 6 | 4 Plex | 681.75 | 35' - 10.75\" | 717.65 | 40' | 721.75 |\n    95→| 7 | 5 Plex | 682.50 | 33' - 4.75\" | 715.90 | 40' | 722.50 |\n    96→| 8 | 6 Plex | 682.25 | 33' - 4.75\" | 715.65 | 40' | 722.25 |\n    97→\n    98→## Block 15: table — This table provides data for various buildings in the project, including building type (e.g., 4 Plex, 6 Plex), gross floor area (sf), building coverage (sf), bedroom type, and number of units.\n    99→\n   100→| Building # | Building Type | Gross Floor Area (sf) | Building Coverage (sf) | Bedroom Type | Units |\n   101→| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n   102→| 1 | 4 Plex | 8,550 | 3,290 | Two Bedroom | 16 |\n   103→| 2 | 6 Plex | 13,004 | 4,997 | Three Bedroom | 23 |\n   104→| 3 | 5 Plex | 10,776 | 4,144 | Total Units | 39 |\n   105→| 4 | 5 Plex | 10,776 | 4,144 | | |\n   106→| 5 | 4 Plex | 8,550 | 3,290 | Maximum Number of Building Stories = 3 | |\n   107→| 6 | 4 Plex | 8,550 | 3,290 | | |\n   108→| 7 | 5 Plex | 10,776 | 4,144 | | |\n   109→| 8 | 6 Plex | 13,004 | 4,997 | Foundation = Slab on grade | |\n   110→\n   111→## Block 16: table — This table summarizes key site data, including zoning, use, site area, allowable and proposed impervious cover, total impervious cover, allowable and proposed building coverage, gross floor area, building slab construction, and common open space required and provided.\n   112→\n   113→| Site Data Table | | |\n   114→| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n   115→| Zoning: | MF-2/Multi-Family Low Density | |\n   116→| Use: Multi-Family Residential | s.f. | acres | % |\n   117→| Site Area: | 94,192 | 2.162 | |\n   118→| Allowable Impervious Cover: | 56,515 | 1.297 | 60% |\n   119→| Existing Impervious Cover: | 61,350 | 1.408 | 65% |\n   120→| Impervious Cover Removed: | 61,350 | 1.408 | 65% |\n   121→| Total Impervious Cover: | 55,502 | 1.274 | 59% |\n   122→| Allowable Building Coverage: | 47,096 | 1.081 | 50% |\n   123→| Proposed Building Coverage: | 32,296 | 0.741 | 34% |\n   124→| Gross Floor Area: | 83,986 | 1.928 | |\n   125→| Building Slab Construction | Slab On Grade | |\n   126→| Common Open Space Required | 4709.62 | |\n   127→| Common Open Space provided | 4710 | |\n   128→\n   129→## Block 17: table — This table shows the site area calculation for MF-2 zoning, detailing the number of units by efficiency, 1 bedroom, and 2+ bedroom, as well as required and provided site area.\n   130→\n   131→| Unit Type | Efficiency | 1 Bedroom | 2+ Bedroom |\n   132→| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n   133→| Number of Units | 0 | 0 | 39 |\n   134→| Required Site Area Per Unit (sf) | 1,600 | 2,000 | 2,400 |\n   135→| Site Area Required (sf) | 0 | 0 | 93,600 |\n   136→| Total Site Area Required (sf) | 93,600 | | |\n   137→| Total Site Area Provided (sf) | 94,192 | | |\n   138→\n   139→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4e1eed96-d749-4c9f-b941-3ae8c1ee87e2","uuid":"c46730a4-9305-4902-b9d5-a393f34066fc","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-09/blocks.md","content":"# Sheet 9 — Content Blocks\n\n## Block 1: drawing — Notes detailing lighting requirements, specifically that all exterior lighting must be full cut-off and fully shielded in compliance with Subchapter E 2.5. It also includes illustrations of fully-shielded light fixtures.\n\nLighting Note\nAll exterior lighting will be full cut-off and fully shielded in compliance with Subchapter E 2.5. All site lighting to be located on the building will be in compliance with Subchapter E 2.5, and will be reviewed during building plan review. Any change or substitution of lams/light fixtures shall be submitted to the Director for approval in accordance with Section 2.5.2.E. See Figure 34.\n[Illustration of four different fully-shielded light fixtures]\nFigure 34\nExamples of Fully-Shielded Light Fixtures\n\n## Block 2: drawing — This block contains a detailed site plan for the Valley View Townhomes project. It shows the layout of multiple buildings, driveways, sidewalks, parking spaces, detention pond, retaining walls, utility lines, and easements. Key features include buildings numbered 1 through 8, various unit numbers, concrete sidewalks, decomposed granite walkways, a mail kiosk, and existing structures to be replaced or retained.\n\nSite Plan showing layout of buildings (Bldg. 1 to Bldg. 8), driveways, parking, detention pond, utility lines, easements, existing trees, retaining walls, and surrounding properties.\n\n## Block 3: notes — A note specifying the required methods for installing fence post holes near a tree's Critical Root Zone (CRZ) to avoid damage, including manual digging and the application of hardwood mulch.\n\nFence Note\nPost holes that are to be installed near a trees Critical Root Zone (CRZ) will be dug by hand. No machinery is allowed in the CRZ and any work done in this area shall be done by hand. Add a 3-inch layer of hardwood mulch in the CRZ prior to commencing construction activities.\n\n## Block 4: notes — Notes regarding accessibility standards and requirements for the project, covering topics such as accessible parking, route cross-slopes, and ramp design.\n\nAccessibility Notes\n1. Approval of these plans by the City of Austin indicates compliance with applicable City regulations only. Compliance with accessibility standards such as the 2010 Standards for Accessible Design or the 2012 Texas Accessibility Standards was not verified. The applicant is responsible for compliance with all applicable accessibility standards.\n2. Accessible parking spaces must be located on a surface with a slope not exceeding 1:50.\n3. Accessible routes must have a cross-slope no greater than 1:50.\n4. Slopes on accessible routes may not exceed 1:20 unless designed as a ramp.\n5. The maximum slope of a ramp in new construction is 1:12. The maximum rise for any ramp is 20in. The maximum horizontal projection is 30 feet for a ramp with a slope between 1:12 and 1:15, and 40 feet for a ramp with a slope between 1:16 and 1:20.\n\n## Block 5: notes — A list of notes related to the release of the site plan, covering topics such as compliance with the approved plan, necessary permits, signage, easements, utility service, demolition, condominium regulations, driveway construction, and right-of-way excavation.\n\na. All improvements shall be made in accordance with the released site plan. Any additional improvements will require site plan amendment and approval of the Development Services Department.\nb. Approval of this Site Plan does not include Building and Fire Code approval nor building permit approval.\nc. All signs must comply with requirements of the Land Development Code (Chapter 25-10).\nd. Additional electric easements may be required at a later date.\ne. Water and wastewater service will be provided by the City of Austin (or identify the service provider if other than the City of Austin).\nf. All existing structures shown to be removed will require a demolition permit from the City of Austin Development Services Department.\ng. No certificate of occupancy may be issued for the proposed residential condominium project until the owner or owners of the property have complied with Chapter 81 and 82 of the Property Code of the State of Texas or any other statutes enacted by the State concerning condominiums.\nh. For driveway construction: The owner is responsible for all costs for relocation of, or damage to utilities.\ni. For construction within the right-of-way, a ROW excavation permit is required.\n\n## Block 6: notes — General notes outlining the engineer's responsibilities, the limitations of the provided utility locations, and the contractor's obligation to verify these locations prior to construction.\n\nRelease of this application does not constitute a verification of all data, information and calculations supplied by the applicant. The engineer of record is solely responsible for the completeness, accuracy and adequacy of his/her submittal, whether or not the application is reviewed for Code compliance by City engineers.\nThe location of all existing utilities shown on these plans has been based upon record information only and may not match locations as constructed. The contractor shall contact Texas 811 for assistance in determining existing utility locations prior to beginning construction. Contractor shall field verify locations of utility crossings prior to beginning construction.\n\n## Block 7: notes — Notes detailing regulations for trash collection on the property, including rules against leaving containers at the curbside unattended and the requirement for visible signage.\n\nTrash Collection Notes\nTrash containers shall not be left at curb side unattended. Signs must be posted on the building or at a location that is publicly visible indicating that trash containers may not be left at curbside. The sign shall indicate the City of Austin's Code enforcement phone number for the public to report violations.\nA restrictive covenant may be required for enforcement purposes.\nReference: TCM, 9.4.2\n\n## Block 8: notes — Information about two benchmarks used for the project, including their location (Mag Nail Set in Asphalt) and elevation in NAVD 88 (GEOID 18).\n\nBenchmarks\nB.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\nB.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n\n## Block 9: notes — The legal description of the property, identifying it as Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision, Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n\nLegal Description\nLot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n\n## Block 10: notes — A table detailing the parking requirements and provisions for the project, including land use, parking spaces provided (surface/garage), compact spaces, ADA spaces, and bicycle spaces.\n\nParking Data\nLand Use Residential\nParking Spaces Provided 10 Surface/ 78 Garage\nCompact Spaces Provided 0\nADA Spaces Required X\nADA Spaces Provided X\nTotal Bicycle Spaces Required 5\nLong Term Bicycle Spaces Required 3\nLong Term Bicycle Spaces Provided 39 (1 per Garage)\nShort Term Bicycle Spaces Required 2\nShort Term Bicycle Spaces Provided 2\n\n## Block 11: other — A graphic scale indicating that 1 inch equals 20 feet on the site plan.\n\n0 20' 40'\nScale 1\" = 20'\n\n## Block 14: table — This table provides data on building heights, including the number of units, building type, average mean sea level (MSL), building height in feet, average building height (MSL), height allowed in feet, and height allowed (MSL).\n\n| Building # | Building Type | Average (MSL) | Building Height (FT) | Avg. Building Height (MSL) | Height Allowed (FT) | Height Allowed (MSL) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 1 | 4 Plex | 686.25 | 35' - 10.75\" | 722.15 | 40' | 726.25 |\n| 2 | 6 Plex | 684.50 | 33' - 4.75\" | 717.90 | 40' | 724.50 |\n| 3 | 5 Plex | 685.00 | 36' - 1\" | 721.08 | 40' | 725.00 |\n| 4 | 5 Plex | 684.25 | 33' - 4.75\" | 717.65 | 40' | 724.25 |\n| 5 | 4 Plex | 682.17 | 35' - 10.75\" | 718.07 | 40' | 722.17 |\n| 6 | 4 Plex | 681.75 | 35' - 10.75\" | 717.65 | 40' | 721.75 |\n| 7 | 5 Plex | 682.50 | 33' - 4.75\" | 715.90 | 40' | 722.50 |\n| 8 | 6 Plex | 682.25 | 33' - 4.75\" | 715.65 | 40' | 722.25 |\n\n## Block 15: table — This table provides data for various buildings in the project, including building type (e.g., 4 Plex, 6 Plex), gross floor area (sf), building coverage (sf), bedroom type, and number of units.\n\n| Building # | Building Type | Gross Floor Area (sf) | Building Coverage (sf) | Bedroom Type | Units |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 1 | 4 Plex | 8,550 | 3,290 | Two Bedroom | 16 |\n| 2 | 6 Plex | 13,004 | 4,997 | Three Bedroom | 23 |\n| 3 | 5 Plex | 10,776 | 4,144 | Total Units | 39 |\n| 4 | 5 Plex | 10,776 | 4,144 | | |\n| 5 | 4 Plex | 8,550 | 3,290 | Maximum Number of Building Stories = 3 | |\n| 6 | 4 Plex | 8,550 | 3,290 | | |\n| 7 | 5 Plex | 10,776 | 4,144 | | |\n| 8 | 6 Plex | 13,004 | 4,997 | Foundation = Slab on grade | |\n\n## Block 16: table — This table summarizes key site data, including zoning, use, site area, allowable and proposed impervious cover, total impervious cover, allowable and proposed building coverage, gross floor area, building slab construction, and common open space required and provided.\n\n| Site Data Table | | |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Zoning: | MF-2/Multi-Family Low Density | |\n| Use: Multi-Family Residential | s.f. | acres | % |\n| Site Area: | 94,192 | 2.162 | |\n| Allowable Impervious Cover: | 56,515 | 1.297 | 60% |\n| Existing Impervious Cover: | 61,350 | 1.408 | 65% |\n| Impervious Cover Removed: | 61,350 | 1.408 | 65% |\n| Total Impervious Cover: | 55,502 | 1.274 | 59% |\n| Allowable Building Coverage: | 47,096 | 1.081 | 50% |\n| Proposed Building Coverage: | 32,296 | 0.741 | 34% |\n| Gross Floor Area: | 83,986 | 1.928 | |\n| Building Slab Construction | Slab On Grade | |\n| Common Open Space Required | 4709.62 | |\n| Common Open Space provided | 4710 | |\n\n## Block 17: table — This table shows the site area calculation for MF-2 zoning, detailing the number of units by efficiency, 1 bedroom, and 2+ bedroom, as well as required and provided site area.\n\n| Unit Type | Efficiency | 1 Bedroom | 2+ Bedroom |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Number of Units | 0 | 0 | 39 |\n| Required Site Area Per Unit (sf) | 1,600 | 2,000 | 2,400 |\n| Site Area Required (sf) | 0 | 0 | 93,600 |\n| Total Site Area Required (sf) | 93,600 | | |\n| Total Site Area Provided (sf) | 94,192 | | |\n\n","numLines":139,"startLine":1,"totalLines":139}}}
{"level":30,"time":1778274502422,"pid":56878,"hostname":"Mac.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":7,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01RWXmvNAMq1XzrYw2b5ywrb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I see important information in Block 5 of Sheet 9:\n\"d. Additional electric easements may be required at a later date.\"\n\nThis suggests there may be electric easements on the property. Let me now review what I know and determine the findings.\n\nBased on my investigation:\n\n**APPLICABILITY CONFIRMED:**\n- Overhead electric lines (OHE) ARE shown on the landscape plan (Sheet 31)\n- Trees ARE proposed near the OHE line (within 0.1-3.2 feet)\n- This is a distribution system (not transmission based on voltage levels typical for townhome developments)\n\n**KEY FINDINGS FROM RESEARCH:**\n\n1. **Trees near overhead electric line:** Vision analysis confirmed several mitigation trees are shown on the landscape plan near the OHE line on the south property line, with distances ranging from 0.1 feet to 3.2 feet.\n\n2. **Species identification problem:** \n   - The mitigation trees (marked \"M\" on the landscape plan) are NOT identified by species on Sheet 31\n   - Vision check confirmed: \"M - MITIGATION TREE\" but no species identification\n   - Sheet 35 has a plant schedule but it's not referenced on the landscape plan\n\n3. **Plant schedule (Sheet 35):**\n   - 2 x Live Oak (Quercus virginiana) - 100 gal, 4\" cal\n   - 10 x Cedar Elm (Ulmus crassifolia) - 100 gal, 4\" cal\n   - 2 x Texas Mountain Laurel (Sophora secundiflora) - 45 gal, 2.5\" cal\n   - 6 x Mexican Redbud (Cercis canadensis var. mexicana) - 45 gal, 2.5\" cal\n\n4. **Utility compatibility:** I need to check if these species are utility-compatible per ECM Appendix F. Based on the grouping guide:\n   - Trees marked \"UC\" in Appendix F are utility-compatible\n   - Large trees are those with mature height ≥ 40 feet\n   - For distribution lines: utility-compatible trees allowed < 15 feet, non-compatible must be > 15 feet\n   - Large trees must be > 25 feet from distribution lines\n\nThe landscape plan lacks clearance dimensions for trees near the OHE line, and species aren't identified on the plan itself, making it impossible to verify utility compatibility or mature heights directly from the landscape documentation. Now I need to evaluate each checklist item.\n\nFor EL-2.1, I cannot verify the species from the landscape plan alone. If the mitigation trees near the OHE are non-utility-compatible, they appear to be within 15 feet, which would be a violation—at best this is not-verifiable. EL-2.2 doesn't apply since no transmission lines are shown. For EL-2.3, I need to check whether Live Oak and Cedar Elm reach 40+ feet at maturity; if they do and are planted 0.1-3.2 feet from the OHE line, that's a clear failure. EL-2.4 is not applicable without transmission lines. Moving to EL-2.5\n\n, there are no transmission easements indicated on the plans. EL-2.6 requires checking if poles exist and whether trees are positioned within 10 feet of them. For EL-2.7 and EL-2.8, I need to verify if pad-mount and underground equipment (transformers and pull boxes shown on Sheet 21) are present and assess tree proximity. EL-2.9 depends on whether root barriers are detailed for trees within 20 feet of underground infrastructure. The mitigation trees on Sheet 31 lack species identification and don't differentiate mature heights—both are confirmed failures under EL-2.10 and EL-2.11.\n\nClearance measurements aren't dimensioned anywhere on the plans, making EL-2.12 and EL-2.13 not applicable. Trees do appear in relation to the overhead electric line on the landscape plan per EL-2.14, though species remain unidentified. Austin Energy approval for restricted zones isn't yet relevant unless other deficiencies trigger that requirement.\n\nI should cross-reference the ECM Appendix F to determine utility compatibility and mature heights for the proposed species, checking if the grouping document contains any relevant reference material.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4187,"cache_read_input_tokens":56179,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4187},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4e1eed96-d749-4c9f-b941-3ae8c1ee87e2","uuid":"d8a4dfd5-cfab-4030-9c9b-fad76169c01e"}
